@@ -117,6 +117,10 @@ Streaming turns create short-lived objects, and macOS compression keeps the rend
 
 Tray image changes cost CPU in both the main process and WindowServer throughout long-running work. Six eased frames at five changes per second preserve the roughly two-second cycle with fewer native image updates. Linux tray images stay panel-sized because StatusNotifier hosts can drop oversized images; monochrome geometric glyphs follow native menu-bar tinting.
 
+### Why the tray is grouped by instance and not last writer
+
+Each app window can show a different instance and pushes its own snapshot, and a window only re-sends when its own snapshot changes. With one shared slot the tray showed whichever window pushed last: a closed window's rows stayed until another window changed, and Allow, Deny and Mark all as read went to the main window even when the row came from another instance, answering against the wrong server. Main therefore keeps a snapshot per window, keyed by the runtime key main recorded for it rather than one the page claims, shows every instance as its own section, and routes each row's action to the window that supplied it. Focus-only ownership was rejected: it hides approvals waiting on an instance in a background window.
+
 ### Why native menu refresh follows content signatures
 
 Tray live state belongs to the renderer and the native controller owns presentation. A signature of menu-affecting data avoids rebuilding native menus for unchanged snapshots without serializing images or handlers; native menu lifecycle events also cover keyboard opening for quota refresh.
