@@ -434,6 +434,39 @@ An exit requested during entry remains queued in Electron even after an explicit
 
 The macOS menu bar item is enabled by default and can be disabled in General settings. The setting applies after restart. While disabled, Desktop skips the native tray controller, tray-specific subscriptions, polling, and quota refresh. Dock badges remain independent: unread activity, session membership, and badge preferences still update the Dock through the shared IPC command. Turning off the Dock badge clears its count without enabling the menu bar item.
 
+Every full app window pushes its own tray snapshot through `desktop_tray_update`.
+Main keeps one snapshot per window, stamped with the runtime key main recorded
+for that window (`desktop_set_window_runtime_key`), and `tray-owner.mjs` groups
+them by instance; `tray-routing.mjs` publishes the result to the tray, Dock
+badge, Dock menu and jump list and routes row actions. A remote host's own UI
+cannot report a key (that command is local-only), so its window takes the key
+of the host main opened it for, else of the configured host whose URL it shows;
+two windows on one host therefore share a section. Windows with no key at all
+are never merged with each other, and a snapshot a window pushed before it
+reported its key is restamped with that key.
+
+The tray menu has one section per open instance, each under its instance name
+with its own approvals and sessions (each section capped at 10 approvals and 8
+sessions); the focused window's instance comes first, the rest in the order
+they first appeared. With one instance the menu is the same as an ungrouped
+one. When several windows show the same instance, the focused one supplies its
+rows, else the most recently focused, else the latest push. The focus event's
+own window counts as focused: while `browser-window-focus` runs,
+`BrowserWindow.getFocusedWindow()` still returns the previously focused window.
+A window with no sessions, approvals or unread rows adds no section. The tray
+icon and tooltip counts, the Dock badge (one count per instance), the Dock menu
+and the jump list cover every section; usage shows the focused instance's. A
+window with nothing to show (a lock or sign-in screen) never leads, so it
+cannot hide the usage section or set the tray's language.
+A closed window, a window that loads a new document (a reload, or an in-place
+host switch even when the new page fails to load), or a window whose runtime
+key changes drops its snapshot; the new document pushes its own.
+
+Each row carries the window that supplied it. Allow, Deny and Open in app go to
+that window; if it is gone, the session opens through the main window with the
+row's runtime key, which hands it to the window for that instance. Mark all as
+read and the menu-open quota refresh go to every window in the menu.
+
