index e2d752f7c..735e77964 100644
--- a/packages/ui/src/components/session/sidebar/DOCUMENTATION.md
+++ b/packages/ui/src/components/session/sidebar/DOCUMENTATION.md
@@ -1872,3 +1872,20 @@ from about 420 to 285 ms; time in tasks over 50 ms fell by 37 percent. What
 still rebuilds the derived indexes at a turn's start and end is the ordering
 store: a session going active and settling again publishes new ranks, and
 group order reads those ranks too.
+
+### Tray rows
+
+The desktop tray and the dock badge list a session exactly when the sidebar gives it a row (maintainer decision 2026-11-24). One module decides that for both: `list/sidebarSessionTree.ts`. The sidebar's collection (`useSessionProjectCollection`) builds its tree from it: which sessions are listed (`buildSidebarSessionTree`, the known-directory filter and the live gap), how they nest (`nestingParentById`, from `resolveSessionNestingParentId`), and which are nested and so not roots (`collectNestedSessionIds`). The tray (`hooks/useTraySync.ts`) builds the same tree and ownership index from the same stores and asks `resolveSidebarSessionRows` which roots have a row. A root has one when it is a managed chat (Chats section), when the ownership index places it in a project, worktree or space group, or, failing both, when it is pinned to the default list (Pinned section). The In work and custom sections draw from the project groups, so they add no session of their own. View state stays out: collapsed groups, search, single-project mode and show-more limits hide a row, they do not decide whether it exists.
+
+What this means in the tray:
+
+- A review session ("Review: X") and a promoted /btw session nest under their original in the same folder, as in the sidebar, so they get no tray row of their own. Their status and unread turn roll up into the original's row, as subagents' status already did. The dock badge counts such a session while its root has a row.
+- A space session whose space mark names no registered project has no sidebar group, so it has no tray row.
+- A session a directory store holds before the global list has it (the live gap) gets a tray row, as it gets a sidebar row.
+- A subagent whose parent is not loaded is a root in the sidebar, so it is one in the tray.
+- A session is nested, and so not a root, when the session it nests under is listed, active or archived. A child of an archived parent therefore has no row of its own while its parent is out of the active list.
+- A project session whose nesting chain loops without reaching a root is listed as a root in its project group (`isInNestingCycle` in `useSessionGrouping`), and the rest of the loop nests under it. The sidebar picks the first member in its own order and the tray the first in tree order, so the loop has one row either way.
+
+The parity test (`hooks/useTraySync.test.tsx`, *tray rows follow the sidebar row rule*) renders one fixture per kind of session the tray meets and checks the tray's rows against both the shared rule and the sidebar's projection. Rejected: a tray-side copy of the rule (`sessionHasSidebarRow`), which had fallen behind on nesting and space ownership.
+
+The tray rebuilds the tree only when one of its inputs changes (global active and archived sessions, projects, worktrees, spaces, OpenCode's projects, the directory stores' sessions): the 5 s poll and flushes for status or quota changes reuse it.
